<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="day-4-notes">
  <title>Day 4 - Sections 2.4 and 2.6a: One-Sided and Infinite Limits</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Last Time:</term> We learned how to find limits of relatively nice functions, using graphs and algebra.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Today:</term> We'll learn about one-sided limits, when limits exist, and how to deal with infinity.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- PAGE 1 -->
  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-warmup-limits">
      <p>
        <term>Problem 1 (Warm Up)</term>
      </p>
      <p>
        The graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> is shown below. Find the following limits. If you aren't sure what the answer should be, discuss with a neighbor.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="55% 40%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/secs2224pic1.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Graph of a piecewise function f(x) showing continuous behavior near x = 2 and a jump discontinuity at x = 0.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <ol marker="1)">
            <li workspace="0.65in">
              <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 2} f(x)</m></p>
            </li>
            <li workspace="0.65in">
              <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0} f(x)</m></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        The first limit above should be straightforward, but the other is a new situation we want to learn about today.
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.25in">
        The issue we see with part 2) of the problem above is also a really important one. What do we do with a limit when it approaches different values from the left and right?
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="def-one-sided-limits">
      <p>
        <term>Definition:</term> A <fillin characters="24"/> limit is a limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x)</m> that only considers the case <fillin characters="10"/> or <fillin characters="10"/>.
      </p>
      <ol marker="i)">
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^-} f(x)</m> is the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> from the <fillin characters="10"/>. It is called the <fillin characters="24"/> limit.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^+} f(x)</m> is the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> from the <fillin characters="10"/>. It is called the <fillin characters="24"/> limit.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <term>Note:</term> We recall that for <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</m>, where <m>L</m> is a real number, the values of <m>f(x)</m> must get infinitely close to <m>L</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> from <fillin characters="15"/> left-hand and right-hand sides.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this reason, the ordinary limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x)</m> is called a <fillin characters="26"/> limit.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <p workspace="stretch"/>
  </page>

  <!-- PAGE 2 -->
  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="fact-two-sided-limit">
      <p>
        Now, the definitions of two-sided and one-sided limits, together with the warm-up problem, lead us to an important fact:
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Fact:</term> The two-sided limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x)</m> exists if and only if (exactly when):
      </p>
      <ol marker="1.">
        <li>
          <p>The <fillin characters="30"/> exists</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>AND The <fillin characters="30"/> exists</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>AND <fillin characters="24"/> <m>=</m> <fillin characters="24"/></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p workspace="0.3in">
        That is, any time our one-sided limits <fillin characters="20"/> match, the two-sided limit <fillin characters="38"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="p-piecewise-one-sided">
      <p>
        <term>One-Sided Limits of Piecewise Functions</term>
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.25in">
        One common place where one-sided limits appear is in <fillin characters="24"/> functions. In such cases, we evaluate the limit using the <fillin characters="24"/> of the function that corresponds to the appropriate side (left or right) of <m>a</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m>. For example:
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-piecewise-limit">
      <p>
        <term>Problem 2</term>
      </p>
      <p workspace="stretch">
        Given that
        <me>f(x)=\begin{cases}e^x-2 \amp \text{if } x \leq 0\\ x^2-\ln(x+1)+1 \amp \text{if } x \gt 0\end{cases},</me>
        find <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0^+} f(x)</m>, <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0^-} f(x)</m>, and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 0} f(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <!-- PAGE 3 -->
  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-graph-infinite-limits">
      <p>
        <term>Problem 3</term>
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, let's examine the following problem and explore the interaction between infinite and one-sided limits a bit. Consider the function <m>f(x)</m> below. Find each limit.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="55% 40%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/Day4fa25pic1.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Graph of a function f(x) exhibiting a jump discontinuity at x = 1, vertical asymptotes at x = -1 and x = 5, with opposite infinite behaviors at x = -1 and matching infinite behavior at x = 5.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <ol marker="1.">
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^-} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1^+} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 1} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -1^-} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -1^+} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -1} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 5^-} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 5^+} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
            <li><p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 5} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p></li>
          </ol>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        So we see that <fillin characters="10"/> of the three two-sided limits (parts 3, 6, and 9) above exist, but the amount of information we can give about them varies, and our final answer is always as informative as possible.
      </p>
      <p>
        We can guess from the graph that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 5} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        But what does it mean for a limit to be <fillin characters="10"/>?
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.3in">
        We say that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = \pm \infty</m> if <m>f(x)</m> becomes arbitrarily <fillin characters="15"/> and <fillin characters="20"/> / <fillin characters="20"/> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="fact-infinite-limits">
      <p>
        <term>Infinite Limits</term>
      </p>
      <p>
        But wait... <m>\infty</m> is not a <fillin characters="34"/>! Our definition says that a limit exists only if it's equal to <fillin characters="34"/>. That means we have an important distinction to make here:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Infinite limits</strong> <fillin characters="38"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is, saying <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = \pm\infty</m> is just a <fillin characters="10"/> <fillin characters="20"/> way of saying that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a} f(x)</m> DNE<mdash/>and we always want to be as specific as possible!
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <p workspace="stretch"/>
  </page>

  <!-- PAGE 4 -->
  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-algebraic-infinite-limits">
      <p>
        <term>Finding Infinite Limits Algebraically</term>
      </p>
      <p>
        We'll spend the rest of the day looking at how we can find limits of this type algebraically<mdash/>they're easy to recognize on a graph, but we want to be able to do both.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let's consider the following limit. First, we can use direct substitution to observe that
        <me>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3}\frac{x^2-9}{(x-3)^2} = \Big( \underline{\hspace{0.6in}} \Big),</me>
        and then applying <fillin characters="14"/> to simplify:
        <me>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3}\frac{x^2-9}{(x-3)^2} = \underline{\hspace{1.8in}} = \underline{\hspace{1.4in}} = \Big( \underline{\hspace{0.6in}} \Big)</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Q:</strong> What do we do with something that looks like <m>\left(\dfrac{\text{nonzero \#}}{0}\right)</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A:</strong> Answers like this are a sign that we have an <fillin characters="14"/> limit!
      </p>
      <p>
        As we saw in parts (4)--(6) and (7)--(9) of Problem 3, to be as specific as possible, an infinite limit has three possible outcomes:
      </p>
      <ol marker="i)">
        <li>
          <p>If the one-sided limits are both <fillin characters="6"/>, the corresponding two-sided limit is <fillin characters="6"/>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>If the one-sided limits are both <fillin characters="8"/>, the corresponding two-sided limit is <fillin characters="8"/>.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>If the one-sided limits are infinite with <fillin characters="20"/> signs (or only one is infinite), the corresponding two-sided limit <fillin characters="10"/>.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Thus our approach in this case will be to evaluate <fillin characters="24"/> limits and see what we can learn.
      </p>
      <p workspace="stretch">
        So let's keep going, looking at the one-sided limits of <fillin characters="18"/> to see what we can learn:
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus, the limit <fillin characters="18"/> <fillin characters="10"/>. And so <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3}\dfrac{x^2-9}{(x-3)^2}</m> <fillin characters="10"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <!-- PAGE 5 -->
  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-big-idea-day-4">
      <p>
        <term>Big Idea of the Day</term>
      </p>
      <p workspace="0.2in">
        Whenever
      </p>
      <ol marker="a)">
        <li>
          <p><fillin characters="14"/> limits are involved,</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><em>or</em> you encounter a limit that you are <fillin characters="8"/> sure how to evaluate algebraically,</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p workspace="0.3in">
        then looking at the <fillin characters="24"/> limits at that point can be a very helpful strategy.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="p-notation-warning-day-4">
      <p>
        <term>Notation Warning</term>
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            When finding a limit, be sure to use <fillin characters="28"/> around <fillin characters="24"/> that involve undefined notations (e.g., <m>0^+</m>, <m>0^-</m>, <m>\infty</m>, <m>-\infty</m>, or <m>0</m> in the denominator) in <em>any intermediate substitution</em>, before arriving at the final answer for the limit.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Points will be deducted on quizzes and midterms for incorrect or improper use of mathematical notation.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Here are some examples:</p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3}\frac{x^2-9}{(x-3)^2} \amp = \Big( \frac{0}{0} \Big) = \text{DNE},</mrow>
          <mrow>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3^-}\frac{x}{(x-3)^2} \amp = \left( \frac{3}{0^+} \right) = \infty, \qquad \displaystyle \lim_{x \to 3^+}\frac{x-4}{(x-3)^3} = \left( \frac{-1}{0^+} \right) = -\infty,</mrow>
          <mrow>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty}\frac{2^{3x} - 2^x + 5}{2^{2x}+7} \amp = \left( \frac{\infty}{\infty} \right) = \ldots \text{(studied later)} \ldots = \left( \frac{1-0+0}{0^++0^+} \right) = \left( \frac{1}{0^+} \right) = \infty,</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{and } \displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty}\frac{x^2-2x+4}{3x^2-5} \amp = \left( \frac{\infty}{\infty} \right) = \ldots \text{(studied later)} \ldots = \frac{1}{3}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
    <p workspace="stretch"/>
  </page>
</handout>